/**
 * Build database-sql/IMPORT-THIS-ONE.sql — the whole database in one import.
 *
 *   npm run make:install
 *
 * WHY THIS FILE HAD TO BE WRITTEN. IMPORT-THIS-ONE.sql already existed and its
 * own header said "GENERATED by scripts/make-install-sql.mjs ... Do not edit
 * this file; edit the part and rebuild." That script was not in the repository.
 * So the one instruction the file gave could not be followed, and the file had
 * silently fallen two parts behind: 7-returns.sql and 8-email-otp.sql were
 * written after it and never folded in, which is why the returns tables and the
 * admin's email code have been handed over as "and run these two by hand" every
 * time. A generated file with no generator is a file nobody can fix.
 *
 * WHAT GOES IN, AND WHAT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT.
 *
 * The parts below are the INSTALL: the tables a shop cannot serve a page
 * without, the catalogue, and the two later migrations that are also plain
 * schema. Every one is idempotent — CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS, ADD COLUMN IF
 * NOT EXISTS, INSERT ... ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE — so importing twice changes
 * nothing and repairs anything missing.
 *
 * 5-missing-variants.sql IS included, and I had it out of this list first on
 * the reasoning that inventing size rows is an inventory decision rather than
 * an install step. That was wrong, and importing into an empty database is
 * what said so: the seed leaves FIFTEEN active garments — twelve in men, two
 * in women, one in outerwear — with no rows in product_variants at all, which
 * is precisely what db-audit.php reports as "sold with NO size rows: the shop
 * shows no size to pick and the order records none". A fresh install failed
 * its own audit. The part completes the seed; leaving it out shipped a broken
 * catalogue.
 *
 * It adds the eight sizes the shop's own size_charts define, at STOCK 0 — the
 * only honest number, since nothing here knows what is on a shelf in Kuwait,
 * and the safe one, because a garment at zero cannot be oversold. The owner
 * types the real counts into /backends -> Stock.
 *
 * (This is a different question from the twenty-seven garments that carry a
 * PARTIAL run — M and L and nothing else. Those already have rows, so this
 * part does not touch them, and whether they are a gap or a real two-size line
 * is the owner's to answer. db-audit.php reports them as a warning.)
 *
 * wallet.mysql.sql IS included too, and finding that out is the other reason
 * this generator earns its place. Its own header says "Fresh installs get this
 * from schema.mysql.sql" — and they do not. Importing the parts into an empty
 * database and diffing the result against the working sandbox is what showed
 * it: wallet_passes, ten columns, present in the shop that works and absent
 * from every fresh install. db-audit.php lists the table as optional, so the
 * only sign was a WARN that reads like a feature nobody switched on.
 *
 * One file in the same folder is NOT here, and its absence is the point:
 *
 *   6-orphan-images.sql  is a guided procedure, most of it commented out, that
 *     ends in a DELETE. It is meant to be read and run a line at a time by
 *     somebody who has looked at the report first. Folding a DELETE into a file
 *     whose whole promise is "safe to re-run" would make that promise false.
 *
 * If a part is added to the folder it must be added to PARTS below on purpose.
 * A glob would have swept both of those in.
 */
import { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs'

const DIR = new URL('../sporta-site/database-sql/', import.meta.url)
const OUT = new URL('IMPORT-THIS-ONE.sql', DIR)
// api/wallet.mysql.sql lives in the docroot rather than here, and that is
// deliberate: package-check.mjs records that the api/*.mysql files are import
// fixtures the owner can reach from the server, with api/.htaccess denying
// *.sql by name. So it is read from where it is rather than moved.
const API = new URL('../sporta-site/public_html/api/', import.meta.url)

// In dependency order. The heading is what a reader sees in the combined file.
const PARTS = [
  ['1-schema.mysql.sql', 'schema — tables, indexes and constraints'],
  ['2-seed.mysql.sql', 'seed — the catalogue and its sizes'],
  ['3-brands.mysql.sql', 'brands — the brands list'],
  ['4-promo.mysql.sql', 'promo — hero slides, settings, promotions and discounts'],
  ['5-missing-variants.sql', 'sizes — the size rows fifteen seeded garments lack'],
  ['wallet.mysql.sql', 'wallet — Apple Wallet passes', API],
  ['7-returns.sql', 'returns — return and exchange requests'],
  ['8-email-otp.sql', 'email OTP — the admin\'s second factor by email'],
  // AND THE SAME DRIFT AGAIN, which is what this script was written to stop.
  // 9-product-brands.sql was generated after the last rebuild and never folded
  // in: a fresh import left brand_slug null on every product, so the brand logo
  // each product card draws had nothing to draw. Checked against the previous
  // combined file rather than assumed — it carried 0 of the 6 `update products
  // set brand_slug` lines, and now carries all 6.
  ['9-product-brands.sql', 'product brands — which brand each garment belongs to'],
  // Read from api/ for the same reason wallet is: the api/*.mysql.sql files are
  // import fixtures the owner can reach from the server, denied to the public
  // by name in api/.htaccess. Moving it here would break that arrangement.
  ['assistantqa.mysql.sql', 'سبورتا AI — the answers the shop writes itself', API],
]

// Repairs, named so that "why is 6 missing" has an answer in the file itself
// rather than in someone's memory.
const NOT_INCLUDED = [
  ['6-orphan-images.sql', 'a guided repair that ends in a DELETE; read it and run it a line at a time'],
]

const head = `-- Sporta — COMPLETE database install, in one file.
--
-- GENERATED by scripts/make-install-sql.mjs (npm run make:install) from the
-- part files that ship beside it. Do not edit this file; edit the part and
-- rebuild.
--
-- HOW TO USE IT
--   hPanel -> Databases -> phpMyAdmin -> pick your database (the one named in
--   api/config.php) -> Import -> choose this file -> Go.
--
-- That is the whole database step. It replaces "import these seven in this exact
-- order", which is seven chances to make one mistake that fails quietly.
--
-- SAFE TO RE-RUN. Every statement is CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS, ADD COLUMN IF
-- NOT EXISTS, or an insert that ignores duplicates, so importing a second time
-- changes nothing and repairs anything missing. It does NOT delete or overwrite
-- existing orders, prices or stock counts.
--
-- Parts, in dependency order:
${PARTS.map(([f, t], i) => `--   ${i + 1}. ${f.padEnd(22)} ${t.split(' — ')[1] ?? t}`).join('\n')}
--
-- Deliberately NOT included — these are repairs, not install steps, and each
-- is run by hand when its own report says it is needed:
${NOT_INCLUDED.map(([f, why]) => `--   ${f.padEnd(24)} ${why}`).join('\n')}

`

let out = head
for (const [file, title, dir = DIR] of PARTS) {
  const body = readFileSync(new URL(file, dir), 'utf8')
  out += `
-- ========================================================================
-- ${title}
-- (${file})
-- ========================================================================

${body.trimEnd()}
`
}

writeFileSync(OUT, out.trimStart() + '\n')

const kb = (n) => `${Math.round(n / 1024)} kB`
console.log(`IMPORT-THIS-ONE.sql — ${PARTS.length} parts, ${kb(out.length)}`)
for (const [f] of PARTS) console.log(`  + ${f}`)
for (const [f] of NOT_INCLUDED) console.log(`  - ${f}  (repair, on purpose)`)
